Land the attribution change UF-04 designed — ONLY IF UF-04 said it is safe to land.

FIRST, read `.rptc/plans/unmeasured-fifth/attribution-design.md` and find the line reading
`RECOMMENDATION-VERDICT:`.

IF IT SAYS `NEEDS-THE-OWNER`, or the file does not exist, or the line is missing or says
anything else: STOP. Change no code. Write two or three sentences into
`.rptc/handoff/2026-09-08-attribution-decision.md` saying which option was recommended, why it
needs a person, and what the owner has to decide. Commit that and finish. This is a complete,
successful outcome — the point of the gate is that a rule change nobody chose never lands.

IF IT SAYS `SAFE-TO-LAND`, implement the recommended option, and treat the control as the
whole job rather than a formality:

  - RUN the control the design names, BEFORE and AFTER, and paste both outputs into the
    commit. A control that was not run is not a control.
  - The failure that matters is silent: a wider suite set inflates scores without anything
    going red, because a module measured beside a sibling that executes it borrows coverage.
    diagnosticsReport reads 43.12% beside its sibling and 41.53% alone — that is the shape.
    So the control must re-measure a SAMPLE under both the old rule and the new one and show
    that no score rose without a coverage reason you can name.
  - If any score rises and you cannot explain it from coverage, the change is WRONG. Revert
    it, write what you found, and stop.
  - Do NOT re-measure or rewrite the 628 existing baseline rows. If the new rule implies they
    should change, that is a NEEDS-THE-OWNER decision by definition — say so and stop.

Then the usual: `npx jest --no-coverage` in full, both typecheckers, whole-repo lint. Several
sop enforcers read the attribution rule (mutation-config-pairing pairs the focus configs, and
the redundancy sweep imports suitesFor from focusModule) — a change here can break them in
ways a scoped check cannot see, so run the FULL suite, not a subset.

RAILS. Stay on the work branch; never checkout or merge develop. NO CLOUD WRITES. Commit only
on a GREEN check with exit codes captured in variables, never read through a pipe. Explicit
pathspec. `Backlog: PL-50` trailer. No attribution trailers.

FINISH by saying plainly which branch you took, and if you landed a change, what the control
proved.
